-- Seed learning material: in-depth, beginner-friendly. For people new to PC building.
-- Updates first 5 guides and inserts 5 new ones. Run once after Guide table exists.

-- ========== 1. How to Build a PC (Step-by-Step) ==========
UPDATE [dbo].[Guide] SET [GuideName] = N'How to Build a PC (Step-by-Step)', [GuideDesc] = N'Assemble your PC safely with clear steps and explanations for every part.', [Content] = N'<h3>Who Is This For?</h3><p>This guide is for anyone who has never built a PC before or wants a clear, step-by-step walkthrough. We explain <em>what</em> you are doing and <em>why</em>, so you understand instead of just copying.</p><h3>Before You Start: Workspace and Safety</h3><p>Build on a <strong>flat, non-carpet surface</strong> (e.g. a table). Carpets build up static electricity; a tiny shock can damage sensitive parts. If you have an <strong>anti-static wrist strap</strong>, clip it to the metal of your case (with the PSU installed and unplugged) so you stay at the same electrical potential as the PC. If you do not have one, <strong>touch the case or a grounded metal object every few minutes</strong> to discharge any static.</p><p>Have a <strong>Phillips screwdriver</strong> (medium size). Keep your components in their boxes until you need them. Read the <strong>motherboard manual</strong>—it shows where every cable and header goes.</p><h3>Step 1: Install the CPU on the Motherboard</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/intel5134.png" alt="A desktop CPU (processor)."><figcaption>What a CPU looks like. It goes into the socket on the motherboard; match the triangle or dot on the corner.</figcaption></figure><p>The CPU (processor) is the brain of the PC. It goes into a socket on the motherboard <em>before</em> you put the board in the case, because the socket is easier to reach on a table.</p><p><strong>What to do:</strong> Lift the small metal lever beside the socket. You will see a tray or the socket pins. Look for a <strong>triangle or dot</strong> on one corner of the CPU and a matching mark on the socket—they must line up. Place the CPU gently; <strong>do not push or twist</strong>. If it is aligned, it will drop in. Then lower the lever and lock it. If it does not sit flat, do not force it; lift the lever and realign.</p><h3>Step 2: Install the RAM</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/consair16.png" alt="A RAM stick (memory module)."><figcaption>What a RAM stick looks like. The notch on the bottom fits only one way into the slot.</figcaption></figure><p>RAM (memory) sticks hold data that the CPU is actively using. Your board has two or four long slots; each slot has a clip at one or both ends.</p><p><strong>What to do:</strong> Push the clips open. Look at the notch on the bottom of the RAM stick—it only fits one way. Line the stick up with the slot and press down firmly and evenly until both clips <strong>snap closed</strong>. You should hear a click. Many motherboards want you to use <strong>slot 2 and slot 4</strong> first (called dual-channel)—check your manual. Repeat for each stick.</p><h3>Step 3: Install an M.2 SSD (if you have one)</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/samsung970.png" alt="An M.2 NVMe SSD (storage stick)."><figcaption>An M.2 SSD is a small stick that plugs into a slot on the motherboard—no cables needed.</figcaption></figure><p>An M.2 drive is a small stick that plugs straight into the motherboard—no cables. It is the fastest type of storage and is ideal for Windows and games.</p><p><strong>What to do:</strong> Find the M.2 slot (a short horizontal slot, often under a small heatsink). Remove the screw at the far end. Insert the drive at a slight angle (about 30 degrees), then press it down and secure it with the screw. Do not overtighten.</p><h3>Step 4: Mount the Motherboard in the Case</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/b760m.png" alt="A motherboard (mainboard)."><figcaption>A motherboard. The CPU and RAM go on here; you then mount the whole board inside the case.</figcaption></figure><p>Now you put the board inside the case. First, install the <strong>I/O shield</strong>—the metal plate that came with the board. It has cutouts for USB, video, and other ports. Push it into the rectangular opening at the back of the case until it clicks. Then look at the case floor: you will see <strong>standoff holes</strong>. Screw in <strong>standoffs</strong> (small brass posts) only where your motherboard has holes—if a standoff is under the board where there is no hole, it can short the board. Lower the motherboard in and screw it to the standoffs. Do not overtighten.</p><p>Next, connect the <strong>front-panel cables</strong> (power button, reset, LEDs, USB, audio) from the case to the small headers on the board. The manual has a diagram; the labels on the wires (e.g. PWR+, PWR-) match the pins.</p><h3>Step 5: Install the Power Supply</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/650consair.png" alt="A power supply unit (PSU)."><figcaption>A PSU. It converts wall power for your PC; the 24-pin and CPU 8-pin cables plug into the motherboard.</figcaption></figure><p>The PSU (power supply unit) converts wall power into the right voltages for your parts. Mount it in the bottom (or top in some cases) of the case. If the bottom has a vent and a fan filter, put the PSU <strong>fan facing down</strong> so it gets cool air. Otherwise fan up is fine. Screw it in from the outside. Then run the <strong>24-pin</strong> cable to the motherboard and the <strong>8-pin (or 4+4) CPU power</strong> to the connector near the CPU socket. These only fit one way.</p><h3>Step 6: Install the CPU Cooler</h3><p>The CPU needs a cooler or it will overheat in seconds. If your cooler did not come with paste pre-applied, put a <strong>small pea-sized dot</strong> of thermal paste on the center of the CPU. Then mount the cooler according to its instructions—usually a backplate behind the board and brackets or screws on top. Connect the cooler fan cable to the header labeled <strong>CPU_FAN</strong> on the motherboard.</p><h3>Step 7: Install the Graphics Card (GPU)</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/rtx4060.png" alt="A graphics card (GPU)."><figcaption>A graphics card. It plugs into the long PCIe slot and needs power cables from the PSU.</figcaption></figure><p>The GPU is the big card that handles games and video. On the back of the case, remove the metal brackets (one or two) that line up with the slot you will use. Push the GPU firmly into the <strong>PCIe x16</strong> slot (the long one) until it clicks. Screw the bracket to the case. Then plug in the power cable(s) from the PSU into the GPU—modern cards need one or two 6- or 8-pin connectors.</p><h3>Step 8: Cable Management and Other Drives</h3><p>Route cables behind the motherboard tray where possible and use zip ties to keep them tidy. If you have a SATA SSD or hard drive, connect a <strong>SATA data</strong> cable from the drive to the motherboard and a <strong>SATA power</strong> cable from the PSU to the drive.</p><h3>Step 9: First Boot</h3><p>Connect your monitor to the GPU (not the motherboard if you have a GPU), plug in keyboard and power. Turn the PC on. If everything is correct, you will see a splash screen or the BIOS. Go into BIOS (often by pressing Del or F2) and check that the CPU, RAM, and drives are detected. Set the boot order so USB or your SSD is first when you are ready to install Windows. Then you can proceed to install your operating system.</p>', [SortOrder] = 1 WHERE [GuideId] = 1;

-- ========== 2. Choosing a CPU ==========
UPDATE [dbo].[Guide] SET [GuideName] = N'Choosing a CPU', [GuideDesc] = N'Understand what a processor does and how to pick one for your needs.', [Content] = N'<h3>What Is a CPU and What Does It Do?</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/intel5134.png" alt="Example of a desktop CPU."><figcaption>What a CPU looks like. It sits in a socket on the motherboard.</figcaption></figure><p>The <strong>CPU (Central Processing Unit)</strong>, also called the processor, is the main chip that runs your computer. Think of it as the brain: it runs the operating system (Windows, etc.), opens programs, does calculations, and tells other parts what to do. When you click, type, or run a game, the CPU is involved.</p><p>For <strong>gaming</strong>, the CPU matters a lot in some games (especially strategy or simulation) and less in others. At higher resolutions (like 1440p or 4K), the <strong>graphics card (GPU)</strong> often does more of the work. For <strong>office work, browsing, and light use</strong>, even a modest CPU is enough. For <strong>video editing, 3D work, or streaming</strong>, a stronger CPU with more cores helps.</p><h3>Key Terms Explained</h3><p><strong>Cores:</strong> A core is like a separate worker inside the CPU. A 6-core CPU can handle six tasks at once (or split one big task). More cores help when you run many programs or heavy software. For gaming, 6 cores is a good minimum; 8 or more helps if you stream or edit.</p><p><strong>Threads:</strong> Some CPUs can run two threads per core (hyper-threading or SMT). That can make the CPU act like it has more “workers” for certain tasks. A 6-core, 12-thread CPU can often do better in multitasking than a plain 6-core.</p><p><strong>Clock speed (GHz):</strong> This is how many billions of cycles the CPU does per second. Higher GHz often means snappier performance in single tasks, but cores and architecture matter too. Do not compare GHz between different brands (Intel vs AMD) directly—design differs.</p><p><strong>Socket:</strong> The socket is the physical slot on the motherboard where the CPU goes. <strong>Intel and AMD use different sockets</strong>, and each generation often has its own (e.g. AM4, AM5 for AMD; LGA1700 for some Intel). Your CPU and motherboard must use the same socket or they will not fit.</p><h3>What to Buy for Your Use</h3><ul><li><strong>Budget or office only:</strong> 4–6 cores (e.g. AMD Ryzen 5 5500, Intel Core i3). Enough for browsing, Office, light use.</li><li><strong>Gaming (1080p or 1440p):</strong> 6–8 cores (e.g. Ryzen 5 5600, Intel Core i5-13400). Good balance of price and performance.</li><li><strong>High-end gaming, streaming, or editing:</strong> 8+ cores (e.g. Ryzen 7, Intel Core i7). Better for heavy multitasking and professional software.</li></ul><p>Always check that your chosen CPU matches your motherboard socket and that your cooler supports that socket. The product page or the cooler box will list compatible sockets.</p>', [SortOrder] = 2 WHERE [GuideId] = 2;

-- ========== 3. Choosing a Motherboard ==========
UPDATE [dbo].[Guide] SET [GuideName] = N'Choosing a Motherboard', [GuideDesc] = N'Learn how the motherboard ties everything together and what to look for.', [Content] = N'<h3>What Is a Motherboard and Why Does It Matter?</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/b760m.png" alt="Example of a motherboard."><figcaption>A motherboard. The CPU, RAM, GPU, and other parts all connect here.</figcaption></figure><p>The <strong>motherboard</strong> (sometimes called the mainboard or mobo) is the large circuit board that everything else plugs into. The CPU, RAM, graphics card, storage drives, and power supply all connect to it. It also has the ports on the back (USB, video, network, etc.) and headers for the case buttons and front USB. Choosing the right board means your parts will fit and work together.</p><h3>Form Factor: Size and Shape</h3><p><strong>Form factor</strong> is the size and layout of the board. It must match your case.</p><ul><li><strong>ATX:</strong> The most common full size. Plenty of room for RAM slots, GPU, and expansion. Fits mid and full tower cases.</li><li><strong>Micro-ATX (mATX):</strong> Shorter than ATX but same width. Fewer expansion slots. Fits many mid towers and smaller cases.</li><li><strong>Mini-ITX:</strong> Small square board. Only one or two expansion slots. Used in compact builds. Cases and power options are more limited.</li></ul><h3>Socket: It Must Match Your CPU</h3><p>The <strong>socket</strong> is the slot where the CPU goes. Intel and AMD use different sockets, and each CPU generation often needs a specific one (e.g. AM4 for older AMD, AM5 for newer; LGA1700 for some Intel). If the socket does not match your CPU, the CPU will not fit. Always check the motherboard product page for “Socket” and match it to your CPU.</p><h3>RAM: Type, Slots, and Speed</h3><p>Motherboards support either <strong>DDR4</strong> or <strong>DDR5</strong> RAM (not both). They have 2 or 4 RAM slots. The product page lists the maximum capacity (e.g. 64 GB) and supported speeds. Buy RAM that matches the type and is within the supported speed.</p><h3>PCIe Slots: For GPU and Add-in Cards</h3><p><strong>PCIe (PCI Express)</strong> slots are the long connectors where you plug in the graphics card and other cards (sound, Wi-Fi, etc.). The longest slot is usually <strong>PCIe x16</strong> and is for the GPU. Boards may have one or more. Newer boards support PCIe 4.0 or 5.0—faster bandwidth for the GPU and NVMe drives. For most users, PCIe 4.0 is plenty.</p><h3>M.2 Slots: For Fast Storage</h3><p>M.2 slots hold NVMe SSDs—small sticks of storage that are very fast. Check how many M.2 slots the board has and their length (e.g. 2280). Some slots share bandwidth with SATA or a PCIe slot, so read the manual if you use several drives.</p><h3>Other Things to Check</h3><p><strong>Connectivity:</strong> How many USB ports (including USB-C)? Built-in Wi-Fi and Bluetooth or not? Ethernet port speed?</p><p><strong>BIOS:</strong> Easy-to-use BIOS helps when you set up memory speed, boot order, or fan curves.</p><p>You do not need the most expensive board for a normal build. A mid-range board that matches your CPU socket and has the ports and slots you need is usually enough.</p>', [SortOrder] = 3 WHERE [GuideId] = 3;

-- ========== 4. Choosing RAM ==========
UPDATE [dbo].[Guide] SET [GuideName] = N'Choosing RAM', [GuideDesc] = N'Understand memory: what it does, how much you need, and DDR4 vs DDR5.', [Content] = N'<h3>What Is RAM and What Does It Do?</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/consair16.png" alt="Example of a RAM stick (memory module)."><figcaption>RAM sticks (memory modules). They plug into the long slots on the motherboard.</figcaption></figure><p><strong>RAM (Random Access Memory)</strong> is the short-term memory of your PC. When you open a program or load a level in a game, the CPU copies data from the storage drive into RAM so it can work with it quickly. The more programs and data you have open, the more RAM you use. If you run out of RAM, the PC has to use the much slower storage as a fallback, which causes stuttering and slowdowns. So having enough RAM is important for smooth use.</p><h3>How Much RAM Do I Need?</h3><ul><li><strong>8 GB:</strong> Bare minimum for light use (browsing, Office). Not recommended for gaming or heavy multitasking—many new games need more.</li><li><strong>16 GB:</strong> Good for most people: gaming, browsing, Office, light editing. This is the sweet spot for most builds.</li><li><strong>32 GB:</strong> Better if you run many programs at once, use heavy mods in games, or do video/photo editing. Future-proof for several years.</li><li><strong>64 GB or more:</strong> For professional content creation, heavy virtual machines, or very large projects.</li></ul><h3>DDR4 vs DDR5 Explained</h3><p>RAM comes in generations. <strong>DDR4</strong> is the older, very common type: affordable and supported by most current motherboards (that use DDR4). <strong>DDR5</strong> is newer: higher speeds and better efficiency, but it needs a DDR5-capable motherboard and CPU, and it usually costs more. For a new build, both are valid; DDR4 is often better value, while DDR5 is better if you want the latest platform.</p><p><strong>Important:</strong> DDR4 and DDR5 are not interchangeable. The notch on the stick is in a different place. Your motherboard supports one or the other—check the product page.</p><h3>Speed and Dual-Channel</h3><p>RAM has a <strong>speed</strong> (e.g. 3200 MHz, 5600 MHz). Your motherboard and CPU support a range of speeds. Faster RAM can give a small boost in games and applications, but the gain is often modest compared to having enough capacity. Do not pay a lot extra for the very highest speed unless you have a specific need.</p><p><strong>Dual-channel</strong> means using two sticks (e.g. 2x8 GB instead of 1x16 GB). The CPU can talk to both at once, which improves performance. Most boards have four slots; the manual usually says to use slot 2 and 4 (or 1 and 3) first for two sticks. So “2x8 GB” or “2x16 GB” is a common and good choice.</p>', [SortOrder] = 4 WHERE [GuideId] = 4;

-- ========== 5. Choosing a Graphics Card (GPU) ==========
UPDATE [dbo].[Guide] SET [GuideName] = N'Choosing a Graphics Card (GPU)', [GuideDesc] = N'Learn what a GPU does and how to pick one for your monitor and games.', [Content] = N'<h3>What Is a GPU and What Does It Do?</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/rtx4060.png" alt="Example of a graphics card (GPU)."><figcaption>A graphics card (GPU). It plugs into the PCIe slot and powers your display and games.</figcaption></figure><p>The <strong>GPU (Graphics Processing Unit)</strong>, or graphics card, is the part that draws everything you see on the screen: the desktop, videos, and especially games. In games, the GPU does the heavy work of rendering 3D scenes, textures, and effects. A stronger GPU lets you run games at higher resolution and quality with smoother frame rates.</p><p>Many CPUs have <strong>integrated graphics</strong> built in—enough for office work and video, but not for modern games. If you want to game, you need a <strong>dedicated</strong> graphics card (a separate GPU you install).</p><h3>Resolution and Settings: What to Aim For</h3><p><strong>Resolution</strong> is how many pixels are on the screen (e.g. 1920x1080 is “1080p”, 2560x1440 is “1440p”). Higher resolution looks sharper but needs a more powerful GPU.</p><ul><li><strong>1080p (Full HD):</strong> Most common. Entry and mid-range cards (e.g. RX 6600, RTX 4060) can run most games well at high settings.</li><li><strong>1440p:</strong> Sharper image. You need a mid to high-end card (e.g. RTX 4070, RX 7800 XT) for smooth gameplay at high settings.</li><li><strong>4K:</strong> Very demanding. High-end cards (e.g. RTX 4080/4090, RX 7900) are typically used, often with slightly lower in-game settings.</li></ul><h3>VRAM: Why It Matters</h3><p><strong>VRAM (Video RAM)</strong> is memory on the graphics card used for textures and frame buffers. If a game needs more VRAM than your card has, you may see stuttering or have to lower texture quality. For new games at 1080p, <strong>8 GB</strong> is a practical minimum; for 1440p and high textures, <strong>12 GB or more</strong> is safer for the future.</p><h3>Power and Case Fit</h3><p>Powerful GPUs use a lot of power. Your <strong>power supply (PSU)</strong> must have enough wattage and the right cables (usually 6-pin and/or 8-pin). The GPU product page lists “recommended PSU wattage”—follow it. Also check the <strong>length and height</strong> of the card: some cases or motherboards may block very long or thick cards. The case specs list “max GPU length.”</p><p>Last-generation cards (e.g. previous RTX or RX series) often offer good value if you can find them at a lower price. Compare benchmarks and reviews to see real-world performance.</p>', [SortOrder] = 5 WHERE [GuideId] = 5;

-- ========== 6-10: Insert new guides (only if not already present) ==========
IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM [dbo].[Guide] WHERE [GuideName] = N'Choosing Storage')
INSERT INTO [dbo].[Guide]([GuideName],[GuideDesc],[Content],[SortOrder]) VALUES (N'Choosing Storage',N'SSDs, NVMe, and HDDs explained for beginners.',N'<h3>What Is Storage and How Is It Different From RAM?</h3><p><strong>Storage</strong> is where your files, Windows, and programs live when the PC is off. When you turn the PC on, data is copied from storage into <strong>RAM</strong> so the CPU can use it quickly. So: storage = long-term, RAM = short-term. Faster storage means Windows and games load faster; it does not usually make games run smoother once they are loaded (that is more about CPU and GPU).</p><h3>Types of Storage Explained</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/samsung970.png" alt="Example of an NVMe M.2 SSD."><figcaption>An NVMe M.2 SSD is a small stick that plugs into the motherboard. SATA SSDs and HDDs use cables.</figcaption></figure><p><strong>NVMe SSD (M.2):</strong> Small sticks that plug into the motherboard (no cables). The <strong>fastest</strong> type of storage. Ideal for Windows and main games. PCIe 4.0 is common; 5.0 is newer and pricier. Your board must have an M.2 slot that supports NVMe.</p><p><strong>SATA SSD:</strong> Connect with two cables (power and data). Slower than NVMe but much faster than a hard drive. Good for extra games or files. Any board with SATA ports can use them.</p><p><strong>HDD (hard disk drive):</strong> Spinning disks. Cheap and large (e.g. 2 TB, 4 TB), but slow and noisy. Fine for backups and media. Not ideal for Windows or games.</p><h3>How Much Space and Which Type?</h3><p>Use at least one <strong>NVMe or SATA SSD</strong> for Windows and main programs (250 GB minimum; 500 GB to 1 TB is comfortable). Check your motherboard manual for M.2 slot count and supported lengths (e.g. 2280).</p>',6);

IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM [dbo].[Guide] WHERE [GuideName] = N'Choosing a Power Supply')
INSERT INTO [dbo].[Guide]([GuideName],[GuideDesc],[Content],[SortOrder]) VALUES (N'Choosing a Power Supply',N'Wattage, efficiency, and why a good PSU protects your whole build.',N'<h3>What Is the PSU and Why Does It Matter?</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/650consair.png" alt="Example of a power supply unit (PSU)."><figcaption>A power supply (PSU). Cables run from here to the motherboard, GPU, and drives.</figcaption></figure><p>The <strong>power supply unit (PSU)</strong> takes electricity from the wall and converts it into the voltages your PC needs (12 V, 5 V, 3.3 V). Every component gets power through the PSU. A good PSU is stable and efficient. A cheap or faulty one can cause crashes or even damage your CPU, GPU, or motherboard. Do not cheap out on the PSU.</p><h3>Wattage: How Much Power Do You Need?</h3><p><strong>Wattage</strong> is how much power the PSU can deliver. Add the typical power use of your CPU and GPU (look for TDP or power draw) and add headroom. For a mid-range gaming PC, <strong>550 to 650 W</strong> is often enough; for a high-end GPU, <strong>750 to 850 W</strong> or more is common. Use a PSU calculator online or the recommended PSU on the graphics card page.</p><h3>80 Plus: What Is Efficiency?</h3><p><strong>80 Plus</strong> ratings (Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum) tell you how efficient the PSU is. Higher rating means less waste and usually better build quality. <strong>Gold</strong> is a good balance. Bronze is acceptable for budget builds from a known brand.</p><h3>Modular vs Non-Modular</h3><p><strong>Modular</strong> PSUs let you connect only the cables you need, which helps cable management. Stick to known brands (e.g. Corsair, Seasonic, be quiet!) and read reviews. Avoid the very cheapest no-name PSUs.</p>',7);

IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM [dbo].[Guide] WHERE [GuideName] = N'Choosing a CPU Cooler')
INSERT INTO [dbo].[Guide]([GuideName],[GuideDesc],[Content],[SortOrder]) VALUES (N'Choosing a CPU Cooler',N'Air vs liquid cooling and how to choose for your CPU and case.',N'<h3>Why Does the CPU Need a Cooler?</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/learning/cpu-cooler.jpg" alt="Example of a CPU cooler (air or liquid)."><figcaption>A CPU cooler keeps the processor from overheating. Add thermal paste, then mount the cooler over the CPU.</figcaption></figure><p>When the CPU works, it produces heat. Without a cooler it would overheat in seconds. The <strong>cooler</strong> (heatsink and fan, or liquid system) pulls heat away from the CPU. A good cooler keeps the CPU at a safe temperature and can be quieter because the fan does not have to spin as fast.</p><h3>Stock Cooler vs Aftermarket</h3><p>Many CPUs come with a <strong>stock cooler</strong>. For light use and low-power CPUs it is often enough. For a quieter PC, lower temps, or a powerful CPU (or overclocking), an <strong>aftermarket cooler</strong> is a good idea.</p><h3>Air Coolers Explained</h3><p>Air coolers use a metal <strong>heatsink</strong> and one or more <strong>fans</strong>. They are reliable and often affordable. Check <strong>case clearance</strong> (max cooler height) and <strong>RAM clearance</strong> so the cooler does not block the RAM slots.</p><h3>Liquid Coolers (AIO) Explained</h3><p><strong>AIO</strong> liquid coolers have a pump on the CPU, tubes, and a <strong>radiator</strong> you mount on the case. They can give strong cooling and a clean look but cost more. Check that your case supports the radiator size (e.g. 240 mm, 360 mm).</p><p>Every cooler is made for certain <strong>CPU sockets</strong> (e.g. AM4, AM5, LGA1700). Your CPU and cooler must match.</p>',8);

IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM [dbo].[Guide] WHERE [GuideName] = N'Choosing a PC Case')
INSERT INTO [dbo].[Guide]([GuideName],[GuideDesc],[Content],[SortOrder]) VALUES (N'Choosing a PC Case',N'Size, airflow, and how to pick a case that fits your parts.',N'<h3>What Is the Case and Why Does It Matter?</h3><figure class="guide-fig"><img src="/hardware/h5flow.png" alt="Example of a PC case."><figcaption>A PC case. The motherboard, PSU, and drives mount inside; good airflow keeps parts cool.</figcaption></figure><p>The <strong>case</strong> holds all your components and determines how much <strong>airflow</strong> your PC gets. Good airflow keeps the CPU and GPU cool so they can run at full speed and fans stay quieter.</p><h3>Form Factor: Size and Motherboard Fit</h3><p>Cases come in sizes that match <strong>motherboard form factors</strong>. A case that supports ATX usually fits smaller boards (Micro-ATX, Mini-ITX). <strong>Full tower</strong> is largest; <strong>mid tower</strong> is most common; <strong>Micro-ATX / small form factor</strong> is compact—check max GPU length, CPU cooler height, and PSU size.</p><h3>Airflow: Why It Matters</h3><p>Components get hot. <strong>Mesh or open front</strong> panels let air in more easily. You want at least <strong>one intake fan</strong> (often front) and <strong>one exhaust fan</strong> (often back). Dust filters on intakes help keep the inside clean.</p><h3>What to Check Before You Buy</h3><p>Match the case to your <strong>motherboard size</strong>. Check <strong>max GPU length</strong> and <strong>max CPU cooler height</strong>. If you use an <strong>AIO</strong>, check radiator support (e.g. 240 mm or 360 mm). Consider cable management space and front ports (USB, audio).</p>',9);

IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM [dbo].[Guide] WHERE [GuideName] = N'How to Install an Operating System')
INSERT INTO [dbo].[Guide]([GuideName],[GuideDesc],[Content],[SortOrder]) VALUES (N'How to Install an Operating System',N'Step-by-step: create a USB installer and install Windows on your new PC.',N'<h3>What Is an Operating System and Why Do You Need to Install It?</h3><p>The <strong>operating system (OS)</strong>—like Windows—is the software that runs your PC. A new PC has no OS until you install one. You do that by booting from a <strong>USB installer</strong> you create on another computer.</p><h3>What You Need Before You Start</h3><ul><li>A <strong>USB drive</strong> (8 GB or more). Everything on it will be erased.</li><li>Another <strong>working PC</strong> and internet to download the installer.</li><li>A <strong>Windows license</strong> (you can add it later).</li></ul><h3>Step 1: Create a Bootable USB Drive</h3><p>For <strong>Windows</strong>: go to Microsoft''s official "Download Windows" page and download the <strong>Media Creation Tool</strong>. Run it, choose "Create installation media," pick language and edition, and select "USB flash drive." For <strong>Linux</strong>: download the ISO, then use <strong>Rufus</strong> or <strong>Balena Etcher</strong> to flash the ISO onto the USB.</p><h3>Step 2: Boot Your New PC from the USB</h3><p>Plug the USB into the new PC. Turn the PC on. Press the <strong>boot menu</strong> key repeatedly (often <strong>F12</strong>, <strong>F2</strong>, <strong>Del</strong>, or <strong>Esc</strong>). When the menu appears, select the USB drive.</p><h3>Step 3: Install Windows (Clean Install)</h3><p>Choose language and "Install now." Enter a product key or choose "I don''t have a product key." Choose <strong>Custom</strong> installation. Select the drive where you want Windows (usually your main NVMe or SSD). If there are old partitions, you can delete them, then select the empty space and click Next. Follow the prompts for region, keyboard, and account.</p><h3>Step 4: After Windows Is Installed</h3><p>Install the latest <strong>chipset and GPU drivers</strong> from your motherboard and graphics card manufacturer''s websites. Then install your games and other software.</p>',10);
